From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 19:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] shaders/colorspace: clamp the Dolby Vision signal to [0, 1]
 before the PQ EOTF

pl_shader_decode_color() decodes a Dolby Vision picture as
R'G'B' = ycc_to_rgb * (YCbCr - offset), then the PQ EOTF, the LMS
matrices of the RPU and the PQ OETF. The EOTF clamped the signal at 0
only. PQ is defined on [0, 1] (SMPTE ST 2084), and above 1 its formula
breaks down:
- from (c2/c3)^m2, about 1.992, the division is by zero or by a negative
  number: inf or NaN, which the LMS matrix spreads to the three
  components of the pixel, and which reaches the output as whatever the
  GPU makes of it;
- between 1 and that, the light is far above 10000 cd/m^2 (314 times at
  1.5, 217000 times at 1.8), and the LMS matrix, which for a BT.2020
  stream is the identity only to about 1e-4 (lms2rgb times the RPU's
  rgb_to_lms, rounded to 14 bits), mixes it into the other components:
  one component above 1 pulls the whole pixel towards white.

Both happen in real streams. On all 2878 pictures of a profile 7 FEL
stream (their active area), with the composer of ETSI GS CCM 001
(libplacebo-9930), R'G'B' reaches 2.22, and 2356 pixels of 35 pictures
are at or above the limit; with libplacebo's reshaping it reaches 1.88.
With either, about 2.8 % of the pixels (491 and 498 million, in 796 and
800 pictures) have a component above 1.

The signal is now clamped to [0, 1] before the EOTF. A component in
[0, 1] is decoded bit for bit as before (clamp() returns it unchanged);
no finite signal gives inf or NaN any more (the divisor is at least
c2 - c3); and a component above 1 is decoded as 10000 cd/m^2, the most
an integer PQ signal can hold, leaking into the other components no more
than a component at 1 does.

Measured on Mesa's software OpenGL ES (zink on lavapipe), on top of
libplacebo-9930, on pictures 44, 45, 47 and 51 of that stream with both
composers: the pixels the clamp changes are pixels with a component
above 1, none other (3026, 3945, 685 and 399 with the composer of the
clause; 3314, 3873, 0 and 800 with libplacebo's reshaping); the picture
stays within one code of 4095 of a 64-bit float reference of the
decoding with the clamp (two or three in at most 117 pixels), as it was
of one without. On picture 45 the 271 pixels that the GPU drew black
(0, 0, 0) without the clamp come out at the top of the range, as in the
reference.

The change is not small where much of a picture is above 1: in 64-bit
floats, on the four pictures of the stream that go furthest (704, 930,
1508 and 1509), 2.0 to 5.9 million pixels change, by up to 2479 to 4095
codes, with either composer. Against the chain with an exact LMS round
trip (each component then only clamped), the decoding without the clamp
is off by up to 2480 to 4095 codes on those pictures, with it by up to
354 to 604: what remains is the leak of the 14-bit rounding into
components near black, which pixels with no component above 1 show too
(up to 415 codes on picture 47) and which this patch leaves as it is.

License: LGPL-2.1-or-later, that of libplacebo and of the file changed,
whose notice stays as it is.
---
 src/shaders/colorspace.c | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/shaders/colorspace.c b/src/shaders/colorspace.c
index 46bd4e4..bf2e350 100644
--- a/src/shaders/colorspace.c
+++ b/src/shaders/colorspace.c
@@ -471,8 +471,12 @@ void pl_shader_decode_color_ex(pl_shader sh,
             .data = PL_TRANSPOSE_3X3(dovi_lms2rgb.m),
         });
 
-        // PQ EOTF
-        GLSL("color.rgb = pow(max(color.rgb, 0.0), vec3(1.0/%f));   \n"
+        // PQ EOTF, of the signal clamped to its domain, [0, 1] (SMPTE ST
+        // 2084): from (c2/c3)^m2, about 1.992, the division below is by
+        // zero or by a negative number (inf, NaN), and between 1 and that
+        // it gives light far above 10000 cd/m^2, which the LMS matrix that
+        // follows mixes into the other components of the pixel
+        GLSL("color.rgb = pow(clamp(color.rgb, 0.0, 1.0), vec3(1.0/%f)); \n"
              "color.rgb = max(color.rgb - vec3(%f), 0.0)            \n"
              "             / (vec3(%f) - vec3(%f) * color.rgb);     \n"
              "color.rgb = pow(color.rgb, vec3(1.0/%f));             \n",
